# Copy to .env — .env is gitignored and is the ONLY place tokens live.
# Everything else (bind address, repos, refresh intervals) is in config.yaml,
# which references these variables by name and never by value.
#
# Classic PAT — required scopes:
#   repo           read issues, including private repositories
#   actions:read   workflow runs and jobs
#   read:org       organisation repositories
#   notifications  the inbox poll used as the change trigger
#
# Fine-grained PAT — repository permissions instead of scopes, and they are
# granted per repository:
#   Repository → Metadata     "Read"            the repo list
#   Repository → Issues       "Read and write"  page 1; write for the planner's
#                                               edit form, labels and comments
#   Repository → Actions      "Read"            page 2
#
# "Read" alone is enough to browse everything; saving an issue then fails with a
# 403 that names the missing permission.
#
# There is no notifications permission to look for here: /notifications only
# supports classic PATs, so a fine-grained token cannot poll the inbox at all.
# Nothing breaks — sections just refresh on their own interval instead.
#
# Mind the repository selection too: a repo you did not tick does not exist for
# the token — even its metadata comes back 404. Missing "Actions" is the usual
# cause of `403 forbidden (check the PAT scopes)` on the Actions page.

GITHUB_PAT=

# A second identity. Add a matching entry under `tokens:` in config.yaml to use it.
# GITHUB_PAT_WORK=

# Semaphore (the `semaphore` widget). The widget names this variable via
# `password-env:`; alternatively use an API token via `token-env:`.
# The password is the one from the Semaphore host — in IaC-Stack:
#   just semaphore-password
# SEMAPHORE_PASSWORD=
